<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nantes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Condevicnum, Portus Namnetum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nantes is a city of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, about 50 km from the Atlantic. Beginning as the Gallo-Roman port of Condevicnum, or Portus Namnetum, of the Namnetes, it became a bishopric and a seat of the dukes of Brittany. In 1598 Henry IV issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a> there, and in the 18th century it was France's leading slave-trading port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nantes.jpg" alt="Nantes" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Brosselin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_depuis_Butte_Sainte-Anne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
